      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Both sides present moral arguments rooted in fundamentally different values: individual liberty versus collective equity. Proponents focus on the moral imperative of parental choice and individualized success, which speaks to opportunity. Critics, however, focus on the moral imperative of equal resource distribution and the community role of public education supporting all students, not just those who can navigate the voucher system. Determining which side has stronger moral ground depends entirely on whether one prioritizes personal freedom of choice or systemic fairness and guaranteed public provision.",
